        "requiredBuilding": "Monument",
		"civilopediaText": [ {"text": "The Acropolis, 'upper city', was the core district of a Greek settlement. Built atop a steep hill, it was designed as a citadel and provided a safe haven to the populace in the event of war. Due to its strong defenses, the acropolis often housed the city’s treasury, as well as the residence of its leader. In peacetime, it was a center for religion and society. Temples were raised to the gods, rituals and festivals were held within its walls, and cultural events such as theatrical and musical performances attracted locals and tourists alike. \nWhile each major city featured an acropolis, the most famous remains that of Athens. Around its base lay the Odeon and the Theater of Dionysus; at its summit, amid temples and shrines, sat the Parthenon and an immense statue of the goddess Athena, surveying her namesake city."}, {"separator": true}],
